version: 2
title: Claude configuration use cases
description: Things I want to do with Claude's configuration, and the mechanism for each. Every event's brief has two items, Claude Code and the chat app, because the methodology differs.
events:
  - key: what-loads
    label: I want to know what a session or chat reads
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Seeing what loads
    detail: Skills, instructions, rules and knowledge docs. Which of them are in context, and from where.
    content:
      key: what-loads
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: What a session reads
        description: The probe. One marked token in every candidate place, one question in a fresh session, and the answer is the map.
        sections:
          - title: Claude Code
            body: |
              Three ways, from empirical to built in.

              **The probe.** `claude-map\probes-place.ps1` appends a marked block to every CLAUDE.md that exists (user, each ancestor folder, the repo) and creates a probe file in every other documented place: `.claude\CLAUDE.md`, `CLAUDE.local.md`, a file named by an `@import` line, an unscoped and a path-scoped rule at user and project level, a subfolder CLAUDE.md, a user and a project skill with one token in the description and another in the body, and a memory topic file plus one line in `MEMORY.md`. Every token carries the same instruction: when asked "probe tokens?", list what you can see and do not go looking. New files are added to `.git\info\exclude`. Then a fresh session in the repo, from each host you care about:

              ```text
              probe tokens?
              ```

              What comes back loaded at start. What does not is waiting for a trigger: skill bodies, the memory topic, scoped rules, the subfolder file. `probes-remove.ps1` takes everything out. Result on 2026-09-20: eleven at start, six on demand, identical in the terminal and the desktop app.

              **`/context`** in a terminal session lists the CLAUDE.md, rules and memory files that loaded, and `/memory` opens them. No probes, but it covers only instructions and memory, only after the fact.

              **An `InstructionsLoaded` hook**, registered in a settings file, fires each time a CLAUDE.md or rule enters context, with the reason: `session_start`, `nested_traversal` for a subfolder file, `path_glob_match` for a scoped rule, `include` for an import, `compact`. A script that appends its stdin to a log gives a continuous record. It does not see skills, memory or knowledge.

              Skills load their description at start and their body on invocation; the probe puts a token in each so the answer shows the split.
          - title: Chat app
            body: |
              No files and no hooks, so the probe goes into the account. Put a token line in each place: Settings > General, a Project's instructions field, a knowledge file uploaded to that Project, and the description of a skill under Settings > Skills. Then three fresh chats: one anchored to nothing, one to the Project, one to a folder holding a probed CLAUDE.md. Ask each:

              ```text
              probe tokens?
              ```

              Expected: the unanchored chat shows the account tokens only; the Project chat adds its instructions and, while the knowledge total is small, the knowledge file; the folder chat settles whether the folder's CLAUDE.md is read. A skill uploaded here can be confirmed from Claude Code afterwards, under `~\.claude\skills\synced`. Remove the tokens by hand where they were set.
  - key: which-memories
    label: I want to know what memory a session has
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Seeing what loads
    detail: Which memories are in context, which are only on disk, and where they are.
    content:
      key: which-memories
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: The memory a session holds
        description: Point at the files, or dump them into the chat.
        sections:
          - title: Claude Code
            body: |
              Auto-memory is a folder: `~\.claude\projects\<key>\memory`, where `<key>` is the repo path with `:`, `\` and `/` replaced by `-`, such as `C--Github-Neogrids`. `MEMORY.md` there is the index and the only part loaded at start, to 200 lines or 25KB; each topic file loads when Claude opens it. So the question has two answers: the index, always; the topics, on demand.

              - `/memory` lists the memory locations and opens any file.
              - `/context` shows what loaded this session.
              - "Recalled N memories" in the interface marks a topic file being read.
              - To dump them into the chat: ask Claude to list and read its memory folder, which it does with its file tools; or a SessionStart hook that prints the index, or the whole folder, as `additionalContext`.

              A repo on the memory-brief regime has no folder at all. Its memory is `claude\memory.brief`, and the hook's outline at session start names every area and every fact. Under `autoMemoryDirectory` the folder is wherever the setting points. Auto-memory is machine-local: a cloud session has none.
          - title: Chat app
            body: |
              Memory lives in the account. Settings > Memory shows what Claude has kept as an editable summary, and a Project has its own memory space seen only from that Project's chats. Asking "what do you remember about me and this project?" lists what is in play for the chat.

              The desktop app kept a per-folder memory for what was a Cowork project in its own database, `%APPDATA%\Claude\local-agent-mode-sessions`, not in a file. Whether that still exists after the merge or moved into the account is the pending test: save a codeword in a folder-anchored chat, then ask for it in a plain chat.
  - key: own-memory
    label: I want my own memory structure
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Memory
    detail: A file of my design holds the memory, a mechanism puts it in front of Claude, and an instruction says how to read it.
    content:
      key: own-memory
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: A memory of your own
        description: Orchestration is the worked example, a brief, a hook, and a paragraph in CLAUDE.md.
        sections:
          - title: Claude Code
            body: |
              **The file.** `claude\memory.brief`: one top section per area, one child per fact, each with a title, a one-line description and a body with Why and How to apply. Any kind the script can parse works; a brief is the natural one, and the Studio edits it.

              **The mechanism.** A hook, registered in `.claude\settings.json`:

              ```json
              "hooks": {
                "SessionStart": [{ "hooks": [{ "type": "command",
                  "command": "node \"C:/Github/orchestration/claude/hook.mjs\" session", "timeout": 15 }] }],
                "UserPromptSubmit": [{ "hooks": [{ "type": "command",
                  "command": "node \"C:/Github/orchestration/claude/hook.mjs\" prompt", "timeout": 15 }] }]
              }
              ```

              The script reads the brief from disk on every call and prints one JSON object; its `hookSpecificOutput.additionalContext` text enters the conversation. At session start it prints the outline, every area with its fact titles. At every prompt it prints the area names. The script can live anywhere; the absolute path in the command is the link. Hooks in a repo's settings file run once the folder is trusted, and `node` must be on the PATH.

              **The reading instruction,** in the repo's CLAUDE.md: memory is in `claude\memory.brief`; take an area's section and read its facts before working there; never write to `~\.claude\projects\*\memory`. The hook repeats the same sentence in its output so it survives a long session. Turn auto-memory off in the same settings file so the two never diverge; the next event says how.
          - title: Chat app
            body: |
              No hooks, so the file has to sit where the chat reads it: as a knowledge file in a Project, uploaded again when it changes, or as a file in the folder for a folder-anchored chat. The reading instruction goes in the Project's instructions field: memory is in memory.brief; before answering, open the area's section and read its facts.

              The outline the hook injects has no equivalent. A large brief is searched rather than read whole, so area and fact titles need to be descriptive enough for search to land on them. Whether a folder-anchored chat reads the folder's CLAUDE.md, which would carry the instruction for free, is still to verify.
  - key: safeguard-memory
    label: I want to control what goes into memory
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Memory
    detail: Nothing written without my knowledge. Turn the automatic writer off, or make every write ask.
    content:
      key: safeguard-memory
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Guarding the memory
        description: Off, ask, or refuse. Three strengths, and only the last is enforced.
        sections:
          - title: Claude Code
            body: |
              **Off.** `"autoMemoryEnabled": false` in `.claude\settings.json` for one repo or `~\.claude\settings.json` for all, or the toggle inside `/memory`. `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` does the same by environment. Orchestration does this and keeps its memory brief instead.

              **Ask.** A line in CLAUDE.md or a rule: before saving a memory, show it and wait for a yes. Orchestration's playbook event "Something should be committed to memory" is the fuller form: show the write, name the area, ask, write only on a yes, quote the write in the report. A UserPromptSubmit hook repeats the reminder every prompt so it holds in a long session. Followed by judgment, not enforced.

              **Refuse.** A PreToolUse hook with matcher `Edit|Write`. Its script reads `tool_input.file_path` from stdin and, when the path is under the memory folder, prints:

              ```json
              {"hookSpecificOutput": {"hookEventName": "PreToolUse",
                "permissionDecision": "deny",
                "permissionDecisionReason": "memory writes go through review"}}
              ```

              or exits with code 2. Without a script, the folder pattern in `permissions.ask` or `permissions.deny` does the same. This is the only hard guarantee, and the same hook can log the attempted write to a review queue instead of refusing it.

              **Review.** `/memory` opens the files, the `modified` field in each file's frontmatter says when it was written, and the consolidate-memory skill merges and prunes.
          - title: Chat app
            body: |
              Settings > Memory turns memory off entirely, and edits or deletes what is kept. A Project's memory space is confined to that Project. An incognito chat writes nothing. An instruction in Settings > General or in a Project, do not remember anything from a chat unless I say remember, is followed but not enforced. There is no hook and no refusal: control is the toggle plus review.
  - key: specific-group
    label: I want to add a specific group
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Scope
    detail: Read as configuration for one part of a project, a group of files or a group of chats, rather than the whole of it.
    content:
      key: specific-group
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Configuration for one part
        description: The narrowest reach. A subfolder or a path pattern in Claude Code, a Project in the chat app.
        sections:
          - title: Claude Code
            body: |
              - **A subfolder's CLAUDE.md.** `dates\CLAUDE.md` loads only when Claude reads or edits a file inside `dates`. For an instruction that always applies in that area.
              - **A path-scoped rule.** A file in `.claude\rules`, or `~\.claude\rules` for me in every project, with a frontmatter:

                ```yaml
                ---
                paths:
                  - "dates/**"
                ---
                ```

                It waits for a matching file; without `paths` it loads at start. Rules are found recursively, so `.claude\rules\dates\` groups an area's files. Also the way to scope by file type, `**/*.sql`.
              - **A skill.** `.claude\skills\<name>\SKILL.md` whose description names the area. Costs only its description until invoked.
              - **Settings** cannot be scoped below the repo; a hook can check `cwd` or the file path itself.
          - title: Chat app
            body: |
              The Project is the group. Its instructions field, its knowledge files and its memory space apply to every chat anchored to it and to nothing else. For one chat only, attach the files and state the instruction in the first message. Nothing sits between a chat and a Project.
  - key: project-wide
    label: I want to promote a kind to project wide
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Scope
    detail: From one part, or from memory, to the whole project. Still mine, or already shared.
    content:
      key: project-wide
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Project wide
        description: The whole repo in Claude Code. In the chat app a Project is already the unit, so the step up is all my chats.
        sections:
          - title: Claude Code
            body: |
              | Kind | From | To |
              |---|---|---|
              | Instruction | a subfolder CLAUDE.md, a scoped rule, a memory | the repo's `CLAUDE.md`, shared, or `CLAUDE.local.md`, mine and git-ignored |
              | Rule | scoped | drop the `paths:` list so it loads at start; it stays in `.claude\rules` |
              | Skill | `~\.claude\skills`, mine in every project | `.claude\skills\<name>` in the repo; there is no personal per-repo slot, so once committed it is the team's |
              | Memory | a topic file | a line in a CLAUDE.md for behaviour, a Studio document for facts; or `autoMemoryDirectory` pointing inside the repo |
              | Settings | `~\.claude\settings.json` | `.claude\settings.local.json`, mine, or `.claude\settings.json`, shared |

              Keep the moved text short: a CLAUDE.md is paid every session. `/context` confirms it loads. The `~\.claude` files go the other way, me in every project.
          - title: Chat app
            body: |
              A Project is already project wide. The step up from a Project to every chat of mine is the account: Settings > General for instructions, Settings > Skills for skills, Settings > Memory for memory. Knowledge files have no account-wide home and stay in a Project.
  - key: team-wide
    label: I want to promote a kind to team wide
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Scope
    detail: From mine to everyone who works on the project.
    content:
      key: team-wide
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Team wide
        description: In Claude Code, team means committed. In the chat app, shared.
        sections:
          - title: Claude Code
            body: |
              Commit it. The team's files are the repo's `CLAUDE.md`, with `.claude\CLAUDE.md` and subfolder ones, `.claude\rules`, `.claude\skills`, `.claude\settings.json` and `.mcp.json`.

              - `CLAUDE.local.md` text moves into `CLAUDE.md`.
              - `.claude\settings.local.json` keys move into `.claude\settings.json`, permissions and hooks included.
              - A user rule or skill is copied into the repo's folders.
              - A memory fact becomes a CLAUDE.md line or a Studio document, or the repo adopts the memory-brief regime, whose brief is a committed file.
              - Check `.gitignore` and `.git\info\exclude` for lines that hide these paths.

              Teammates see permission allow rules, `additionalDirectories`, marketplaces and hooks only after they trust the folder, and an `@import` that resolves outside the repo asks each of them for approval once. A skill shared across several repos is a plugin, published in a marketplace and named in `.claude\settings.json` under `extraKnownMarketplaces` and `enabledPlugins`.
          - title: Chat app
            body: |
              Share the Project: its instructions, knowledge files and memory space reach everyone it is shared with.

              Share the skill, from its page under Settings > Skills. Two ways on a Team plan: with named people, who get it by link, or published to the organisation's directory, where anyone can find and install it; an owner must turn on Share with organization under Organization settings > Skills before the publish option appears, and Team defaults publishing to Open, with no review step. Either way the skill is view-only for recipients and stays off until each of them enables it. When the owner saves a new version, everyone who has it gets the update at next use, and the owner can withdraw access at any time.

              A teammate's Claude Code syncs the skill into `~\.claude\skills\synced` once they have enabled it, checking claude.ai about every ten minutes, in sessions signed in with the claude.ai account rather than an API key. So a shared skill updates everywhere from one place, where a committed skill updates only when each person pulls.
  - key: org-wide
    label: I want to promote a kind to org wide
    trigger: chosen
    domain: Scope
    detail: From one team to every machine and account the organisation manages.
    content:
      key: org-wide
      kind: brief
      doc:
        title: Org wide
        description: An admin deploys it, and nothing a developer sets overrides it.
        sections:
          - title: Claude Code
            body: |
              - **Instruction.** A managed CLAUDE.md at `C:\Program Files\ClaudeCode\CLAUDE.md` on Windows, `/Library/Application Support/ClaudeCode/CLAUDE.md` on macOS, `/etc/claude-code/CLAUDE.md` on Linux, distributed by MDM or Group Policy; or the `claudeMd` key inside `managed-settings.json`. Loads before user and project files and cannot be excluded.
              - **Settings.** `managed-settings.json` in the same folder, an MDM policy, or server-managed settings from the claude.ai admin console. Highest precedence. Enforcement belongs here: `permissions.deny`, `sandbox`, `env`, `forceLoginOrgUUID`. Only server-managed settings reach cloud sessions.
              - **Skills and rules.** A plugin in a marketplace the managed settings declare, so every machine installs it; or skills provisioned on claude.ai, which sync down to each user's `~\.claude\skills\synced`.
              - **Memory.** No org mechanism. What the org wants remembered is an instruction.

              `/status` names the managed source in force on a machine.
          - title: Chat app
            body: |
              Provision the skill: an owner uploads or creates it under Organization settings > Skills. It appears for everyone automatically and is on by default; a user can switch it off for themselves but cannot delete it, and an update by the owner is the update for all. This is the only route that reaches everyone without each person enabling something. A skill merely shared or published to the directory has org reach but stays opt-in per person, which is the team tier. Provisioned skills show in chat, in the desktop app's Chat tab and in Cowork, and sync down to every member's Claude Code.

              Beyond skills, the admin console holds org-level settings and policies, and a Project can be shared with the whole organisation. Custom instructions and memory stay per user, so an org-wide behaviour is a provisioned skill or a shared Project's instructions.
